  from jsonb_array_elements('[{"n":1,"t":"single_choice","p":"A teacher records five variables for each of the 40 students in her class in Tamale: I favourite fruit; II travel time to school, in minutes; III number of brothers and sisters; IV shoe size; V the market where the family usually shops. Which of these variables are categorical (qualitative)?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A teacher records five variables for each of the 40 students in her class in Tamale: I favourite fruit; II travel time to school, in minutes; III number of brothers and sisters; IV shoe size; V the market where the family usually shops. Which of these variables are categorical (qualitative)?"}]}]},"e":"Favourite fruit (I) and the family’s market (V) are labels, not numbers, so both are categorical. Travel time (II), number of siblings (III) and shoe size (IV) are numerical measurements, so they are quantitative.\\n\\nAnswer: B","m":1,"df":"easy","tp":"Categorising Data and Stem-and-Leaf Plots","cfg":{"source":"myp4-full-formal-assessment-2026","question_number":1,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"A","next_time_hint":"Sort each variable into ‘label’ (words) or ‘number’ before you choose.","assessment_style":"formal_assessment","option_labels":["II and III only","I and V only","III, IV and V only","I, II and V only"],"option_key":[2],"hints":["Read variables I to V one at a time and ask: is this a label or a number?","Only variables made of words rather than numbers are categorical."]},"marking":""},{"n":2,"t":"single_choice","p":"The stem-and-leaf plot shows the marks, out of 80, obtained by 22 students in a mathematics test. How many students scored at least 50 marks?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The stem-and-leaf plot shows the marks, out of 80, obtained by 22 students in a mathematics test. How many students scored at least 50 marks?"}]},{"type":"image","attrs":{"src":"/exam-figures/myp4/q02_stemleaf.jpg","alt":"Stem-and-leaf plot of the marks of 22 students","width":96}}]},"e":"Scores of at least 50 sit on stems 5, 6 and 7. Stem 5 has 0 1 3 3 4 6 = 6 leaves. Stem 6 has 0 2 = 2 leaves. Stem 7 has 1 = 1 leaf. Total 6 + 2 + 1 = 9.\\n\\nAnswer: D","m":1,"df":"easy","tp":"Categorising Data and Stem-and-Leaf Plots","cfg":{"source":"myp4-full-formal-assessment-2026","question_number":2,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"A","next_time_hint":"Count the leaves on stems 5, 6 and 7.","assessment_style":"formal_assessment","option_labels":["6","8","14","9"],"option_key":[4],"hints":["‘At least 50’ means stem 5 and everything above it.","Count the leaves on stems 5, 6 and 7 and add them."],"figure":"myp4_q02_stemleaf"},"marking":""},{"n":3,"t":"single_choice","p":"A trader at Agbogbloshie records four quantities for each crate of tomatoes she sells. Which one of these is a continuous quantitative variable?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A trader at Agbogbloshie records four quantities for each crate of tomatoes she sells. Which one of these is a continuous quantitative variable?"}]}]},"e":"Mass can take any value on a scale — 12.4 kg, 12.45 kg — so it is continuous. The number of tomatoes and the number of days are counts (discrete); the colour grade is a label (categorical).\\n\\nAnswer: D","m":1,"df":"medium","tp":"Categorising Data and Stem-and-Leaf Plots","cfg":{"source":"myp4-full-formal-assessment-2026","question_number":3,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"A","next_time_hint":"Ask: could this variable take any value, or only whole counts?","assessment_style":"formal_assessment","option_labels":["The number of tomatoes in the crate","The colour grade of the tomatoes (red, orange, green)","The number of days the crate has been stored","The mass of the crate, in kilograms"],"option_key":[4],"hints":["Continuous variables are measured, not counted.","Mass in kilograms can carry decimals — counts of tomatoes cannot."]},"marking":""},{"n":4,"t":"single_choice","p":"The plot shows the heights of 22 fifteen-year-old students, measured in centimetres, but the key was left off the page. Which key makes the data possible?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The plot shows the heights of 22 fifteen-year-old students, measured in centimetres, but the key was left off the page. Which key makes the data possible?"}]},{"type":"image","attrs":{"src":"/exam-figures/myp4/q04_heights.jpg","alt":"Stem-and-leaf plot of student heights with a missing key","width":96}}]},"e":"Fifteen-year-old students are about 140–190 cm tall. Stems 14–18 with a single leaf fit hundreds of centimetres: 152 cm is sensible. 15.2 cm and 15.2 m are implausible, and 152 mm = 15.2 cm is also absurd for a student.\\n\\nAnswer: A","m":1,"df":"medium","tp":"Categorising Data and Stem-and-Leaf Plots","cfg":{"source":"myp4-full-formal-assessment-2026","question_number":4,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"B","next_time_hint":"Think about the height of an actual fifteen-year-old.","assessment_style":"formal_assessment","option_labels":["15 | 2 means 152 cm","15 | 2 means 15.2 cm","15 | 2 means 15.2 m","15 | 2 means 152 mm"],"option_key":[1],"hints":["Estimate a realistic height for a 15-year-old before reading the stems.","Convert each option to ordinary units and see which is plausible."],"figure":"myp4_q04_heights"},"marking":""},{"n":5,"t":"single_choice","p":"This back-to-back stem-and-leaf plot shows the daily mass of tomatoes sold, in kilograms, at two market stalls over 12 market days each. What is the median daily sale at stall A?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"This back-to-back stem-and-leaf plot shows the daily mass of tomatoes sold, in kilograms, at two market stalls over 12 market days each. What is the median daily sale at stall A?"}]},{"type":"image","attrs":{"src":"/exam-figures/myp4/q05_tomatoes.jpg","alt":"Back-to-back stem-and-leaf plot of daily tomato sales at two stalls","width":96}}]},"e":"Stall A’s leaves (right-hand side), in order: 12, 15, 18, 21, 24, 26, 28, 31, 33, 35, 38, 42. With 12 values the median is the mean of the 6th and 7th: (26 + 28) ÷ 2 = 27 kg.\\n\\nAnswer: D","m":1,"df":"hard","tp":"Categorising Data and Stem-and-Leaf Plots","cfg":{"source":"myp4-full-formal-assessment-2026","question_number":5,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"B","next_time_hint":"Read only the right-hand (stall A) leaves and order them.","assessment_style":"formal_assessment","option_labels":["28 kg","26.9 kg","26 kg","27 kg"],"option_key":[4],"hints":["Ignore stall B’s side of the plot entirely.","Write out stall A’s 12 values in order and average the 6th and 7th."],"figure":"myp4_q05_tomatoes"},"marking":""},{"n":6,"t":"single_choice","p":"A student copied these 20 numbers — customers served each day at a salon in East Legon — into a stem-and-leaf plot, but she wrote one number in the wrong row. Which number is incorrectly represented in her plot?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A student copied these 20 numbers — customers served each day at a salon in East Legon — into a stem-and-leaf plot, but she wrote one number in the wrong row. Which number is incorrectly represented in her plot?"}]},{"type":"image","attrs":{"src":"/exam-figures/myp4/q06_salon.jpg","alt":"The student’s copied stem-and-leaf plot","width":96}}]},"e":"The copied plot holds twenty leaves: stem 1 has 8; stem 2 has 2, 5; stem 3 has 1, 4, 6, 8; stem 4 has 0, 2, 2, 5, 7, 8; stem 5 has 5, 8; stem 6 has 0, 3, 6; stem 7 has 1, 4. The row-4 leaves contain a duplicated 2 (‘…0 2 2 5…’) and there is no 2 anywhere on stem 5. The only value that fits this pattern is 52, miswritten as 42. 48, 42 and 55 all sit correctly on their printed rows. (The original 20-number list is not reproduced in the paper; the misplacement is identified from the plot itself.)\\n\\nAnswer: B","m":1,"df":"hard","tp":"Categorising Data and Stem-and-Leaf Plots","cfg":{"source":"myp4-full-formal-assessment-2026","question_number":6,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"C","next_time_hint":"Count the leaves in each row and look for the duplicated/missing value.","assessment_style":"formal_assessment","option_labels":["48","52","55","42"],"option_key":[2],"hints":["Compare the leaves shown with what each stem should hold.","Which row of the plot disagrees with the list of marks given in the question?"],"figure":"myp4_q06_salon"},"marking":""},{"n":7,"t":"single_choice","p":"The plot shows the marks, out of 70, of 25 students in a spelling test. A student is asked to find Q1. What is the correct value?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The plot shows the marks, out of 70, of 25 students in a spelling test. A student is asked to find Q1. What is the correct value?"}]},{"type":"image","attrs":{"src":"/exam-figures/myp4/q07_spelling.jpg","alt":"Stem-and-leaf plot of spelling test marks of 25 students","width":96}}]},"e":"Ordered leaves: 12, 15, 18, 21, 23, 25, 28, 30, 32, 34, 35, 37, 38, 40, 41, 42, 44, 45, 46, 47, 48, 49, 50, 52, 55 (n = 25). The lower half for the quartile is the first 12 values: 12, 15, 18, 21, 23, 25, 28, 30, 32, 34, 35, 37. Q1 = (25 + 28) ÷ 2 = 26.5.\\n\\nAnswer: B","m":1,"df":"easy","tp":"Quartiles, Range and Interquartile Range","cfg":{"source":"myp4-full-formal-assessment-2026","question_number":7,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"A","next_time_hint":"Find the lower half of the data first.","assessment_style":"formal_assessment","option_labels":["38","26.5","25","28"],"option_key":[2],"hints":["First write out all 25 marks in order from the plot.","Q1 is the median of the 12 lowest marks — average the 6th and 7th of those."],"figure":"myp4_q07_spelling"},"marking":""},{"n":8,"t":"single_choice","p":"Ten students recorded the time, in minutes, they spent travelling to school: 30, 18, 44, 25, 50, 34, 22, 40, 28, 36. Which is the correct five-point summary?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Ten students recorded the time, in minutes, they spent travelling to school: 30, 18, 44, 25, 50, 34, 22, 40, 28, 36. Which is the correct five-point summary?"}]}]},"e":"Sorted: 18, 22, 25, 28, 30, 34, 36, 40, 44, 50. Min 18, max 50. Median = (30 + 34) ÷ 2 = 32 (average of the 5th and 6th values). Lower half 18, 22, 25, 28, 30 → Q1 = 25. Upper half 34, 36, 40, 44, 50 → Q3 = 40. Five-point summary: 18, 25, 32, 40, 50.\\n\\nAnswer: C","m":1,"df":"easy","tp":"Quartiles, Range and Interquartile Range","cfg":{"source":"myp4-full-formal-assessment-2026","question_number":8,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"A","next_time_hint":"Order the data before touching the quartiles.","assessment_style":"formal_assessment","option_labels":["18, 26.5, 32, 38, 50","22, 25, 32, 40, 44","18, 25, 32, 40, 50","18, 25, 30, 40, 50"],"option_key":[3],"hints":["Sort the ten times from smallest to largest.","Q1 and Q3 are the medians of the two halves; the median is the middle pair averaged."]},"marking":""},{"n":9,"t":"single_choice","p":"A cooperative recorded the maize yield, in kilograms per plot, on 12 farms: 42, 45, 48, 51, 55, 58, 60, 63, 66, 70, 74, 88. How much greater is the range than the interquartile range? Give your answer to 1 decimal place.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A cooperative recorded the maize yield, in kilograms per plot, on 12 farms: 42, 45, 48, 51, 55, 58, 60, 63, 66, 70, 74, 88. How much greater is the range than the interquartile range? Give your answer to 1 decimal place."}]}]},"e":"Sorted: 42, 45, 48, 51, 55, 58, 60, 63, 66, 70, 74, 88. Range = 88 − 42 = 46. Lower half 42, 45, 48, 51, 55, 58 → Q1 = (48 + 51) ÷ 2 = 49.5. Upper half 60, 63, 66, 70, 74, 88 → Q3 = (66 + 70) ÷ 2 = 68. IQR = 18.5. Range − IQR = 46 − 18.5 = 27.5.\\n\\nAnswer: C","m":1,"df":"medium","tp":"Quartiles, Range and Interquartile Range","cfg":{"source":"myp4-full-formal-assessment-2026","question_number":9,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"A","next_time_hint":"Compute the range and the IQR separately, then subtract.","assessment_style":"formal_assessment","option_labels":["46","20","27.5","32"],"option_key":[3],"hints":["Range = largest − smallest.","IQR = Q3 − Q1 with each half taken from the ordered list."]},"marking":""},{"n":10,"t":"single_choice","p":"A data set contains 21 different values. A student finds the quartiles by putting the median into both halves instead of excluding it. What is the effect of this error?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A data set contains 21 different values. A student finds the quartiles by putting the median into both halves instead of excluding it. What is the effect of this error?"}]}]},"e":"Example: values 1 to 21. Correct: lower half 1–10 → Q1 = 5.5; upper half 12–21 → Q3 = 16.5; IQR = 11. With the median 11 counted in both halves: lower half 1–11 → Q1 = 6 (larger); upper half 11–21 → Q3 = 16 (smaller); IQR = 10 (smaller).\\n\\nAnswer: A","m":1,"df":"medium","tp":"Quartiles, Range and Interquartile Range","cfg":{"source":"myp4-full-formal-assessment-2026","question_number":10,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"C","next_time_hint":"Test the claim with the data set 1, 2, …, 21.","assessment_style":"formal_assessment","option_labels":["Q1 becomes larger and Q3 becomes smaller, so the IQR becomes smaller.","Q1 becomes smaller and Q3 becomes larger, so the IQR becomes larger.","Both Q1 and Q3 become larger, so the IQR is unchanged.","There is no effect, because the median is the middle value either way."],"option_key":[1],"hints":["Try it on 1, 2, 3, …, 21 and compare both methods.","Including the median pulls each quartile one position towards the centre."]},"marking":""},{"n":11,"t":"single_choice","p":"The table below lists the daily temperatures, in degrees Celsius, recorded in Kumasi for 12 days. A meteorologist adds 5 to every value to correct a faulty thermometer. Which statement describes the effect?\\n\\nRecorded temperatures (°C): 46, 52, 55, 61, 63, 68, 72, 75, 81, 88, 90, 96","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The table below lists the daily temperatures, in degrees Celsius, recorded in Kumasi for 12 days. A meteorologist adds 5 to every value to correct a faulty thermometer. Which statement describes the effect?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Recorded temperatures (°C): 46, 52, 55, 61, 63, 68, 72, 75, 81, 88, 90, 96"}]}]},"e":"Adding a constant shifts every value by the same amount: the mean and the median both rise by 5. Differences between values are untouched, so the range and the IQR are unchanged (shifting data changes location, not spread).\\n\\nAnswer: A","m":1,"df":"hard","tp":"Quartiles, Range and Interquartile Range","cfg":{"source":"myp4-full-formal-assessment-2026","question_number":11,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"C","next_time_hint":"Adding a constant changes location, not spread.","assessment_style":"formal_assessment","option_labels":["The mean and the median both increase by 5, while the range and the IQR stay the same.","The median increases by 5 but the mean stays the same, because only the middle value changed.","The mean and the median both increase by 5, and so do the range and the IQR.","The IQR increases by 5 because every value in the middle half of the data increased."],"option_key":[1],"hints":["What happens to each value when you add 5 to all of them?","Ranges and IQRs are differences — what does a common shift do to a difference?"]},"marking":""},{"n":12,"t":"single_choice","p":"Two athletes recorded the number of shuttle-run completions per session over 12 sessions. Their five-point summaries are drawn as box plots below. Which conclusion is valid?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Two athletes recorded the number of shuttle-run completions per session over 12 sessions. Their five-point summaries are drawn as box plots below. Which conclusion is valid?"}]},{"type":"image","attrs":{"src":"/exam-figures/myp4/q12_shuttle.jpg","alt":"Box plots of shuttle-run completions for athletes A and B","width":96}}]},"e":"Reading the box plots: A’s median sits near 65 and B’s near 71, so B’s median is higher. A’s box spans roughly 59 to 71 (IQR ≈ 12) while B’s box spans roughly 62 to 82 (IQR ≈ 20), so A’s IQR is smaller and A is more consistent. Consistency is judged by spread, not by how high the maximum reached.\\n\\nAnswer: B","m":1,"df":"hard","tp":"Quartiles, Range and Interquartile Range","cfg":{"source":"myp4-full-formal-assessment-2026","question_number":12,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"D","next_time_hint":"Compare medians, then compare the widths of the boxes.","assessment_style":"formal_assessment","option_labels":["B is more consistent because B reached a higher maximum.","B has the higher median, but A is the more consistent performer because A’s IQR is smaller.","The two athletes are equally consistent because both data sets contain 12 sessions.","A has the higher median and is more consistent, because A’s range is smaller."],"option_key":[2],"hints":["Read the median lines and the box widths from the plots.","‘More consistent’ means smaller spread — use the IQR."],"figure":"myp4_q12_shuttle"},"marking":""},{"n":13,"t":"single_choice","p":"The box plot shows the time, in minutes, that 14 students take to walk to the bus station. What is the interquartile range of these times?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The box plot shows the time, in minutes, that 14 students take to walk to the bus station. What is the interquartile range of these times?"}]},{"type":"image","attrs":{"src":"/exam-figures/myp4/q13_walk.jpg","alt":"Box plot of 14 students’ walking times","width":96}}]},"e":"From the box: Q1 ≈ 15 minutes and Q3 ≈ 30 minutes (the left and right edges of the box). IQR = 30 − 15 = 15 minutes.\\n\\nAnswer: B","m":1,"df":"easy","tp":"Box-and-Whisker Plots and Outliers","cfg":{"source":"myp4-full-formal-assessment-2026","question_number":13,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"A","next_time_hint":"The IQR is the width of the box.","assessment_style":"formal_assessment","option_labels":["34 minutes","15 minutes","24 minutes","6 minutes"],"option_key":[2],"hints":["Read where the box starts and where it ends on the time axis.","IQR = Q3 − Q1; subtract the left edge of the box from the right edge."],"figure":"myp4_q13_walk"},"marking":""},{"n":14,"t":"single_choice","p":"A box plot is drawn for the 100 m times, in seconds, of all 40 athletes in a school race. How many athletes are represented by the box itself (the part between Q1 and Q3)?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A box plot is drawn for the 100 m times, in seconds, of all 40 athletes in a school race. How many athletes are represented by the box itself (the part between Q1 and Q3)?"}]}]},"e":"The box holds the middle half of the data — 50% of all values. 50% of 40 athletes = 20 athletes.\\n\\nAnswer: C","m":1,"df":"easy","tp":"Box-and-Whisker Plots and Outliers","cfg":{"source":"myp4-full-formal-assessment-2026","question_number":14,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"A","next_time_hint":"The box is the middle half of the data.","assessment_style":"formal_assessment","option_labels":["30","10","20","40"],"option_key":[3],"hints":["What fraction of the data lies between Q1 and Q3?","Half of 40 is 20."]},"marking":""},{"n":15,"t":"single_choice","p":"Twelve torch batteries of the same brand were tested to failure, giving lifetimes in hours: 42, 45, 48, 50, 52, 54, 56, 58, 60, 63, 66, 84. Using the 1.5 × IQR rule, what is the upper fence, and is the 84-hour battery an outlier? Give the fence to 2 decimal places.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Twelve torch batteries of the same brand were tested to failure, giving lifetimes in hours: 42, 45, 48, 50, 52, 54, 56, 58, 60, 63, 66, 84. Using the 1.5 × IQR rule, what is the upper fence, and is the 84-hour battery an outlier? Give the fence to 2 decimal places."}]},{"type":"image","attrs":{"src":"/exam-figures/myp4/q15_batteries.jpg","alt":"Box plot of battery lifetimes with the 84-hour battery marked","width":96}}]},"e":"Sorted values are already in order (n = 12). Q1 = (48 + 50) ÷ 2 = 49 hours. Upper half 56, 58, 60, 63, 66, 84 → Q3 = (60 + 63) ÷ 2 = 61.5 hours. IQR = 61.5 − 49 = 12.5 hours. Upper fence = Q3 + 1.5 × IQR = 61.5 + 18.75 = 80.25 hours. Since 84 > 80.25, the 84-hour battery is an outlier.\\n\\nAnswer: D","m":1,"df":"medium","tp":"Box-and-Whisker Plots and Outliers","cfg":{"source":"myp4-full-formal-assessment-2026","question_number":15,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"A","next_time_hint":"Find Q1 and Q3 first, then the fence.","assessment_style":"formal_assessment","option_labels":["Upper fence 74 h; 84 h is an outlier.","Upper fence 124.5 h; 84 h is not an outlier.","Upper fence 67.75 h; 84 h is not an outlier.","Upper fence 80.25 h; 84 h is an outlier."],"option_key":[4],"hints":["Split the ordered list in two halves to get Q1 and Q3.","Upper fence = Q3 + 1.5 × IQR; compare 84 with it."],"figure":"myp4_q15_batteries"},"marking":""},{"n":16,"t":"single_choice","p":"The two box plots show the waiting time, in minutes, of 12 customers at each of two market stalls. Which statement is a valid conclusion from the plots?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The two box plots show the waiting time, in minutes, of 12 customers at each of two market stalls. Which statement is a valid conclusion from the plots?"}]},{"type":"image","attrs":{"src":"/exam-figures/myp4/q16_stalls.jpg","alt":"Box plots of waiting times at stalls A and B","width":96}}]},"e":"Stall B’s median (≈ 41 minutes) is much higher than stall A’s (≈ 19 minutes), and stall B’s box is far wider (IQR ≈ 40 minutes vs ≈ 8 minutes). So on average customers at B wait longer and B’s times vary more. A larger IQR does not force a larger median — that option confuses spread with centre — and a shared axis scale says nothing about spread.\\n\\nAnswer: B","m":1,"df":"medium","tp":"Box-and-Whisker Plots and Outliers","cfg":{"source":"myp4-full-formal-assessment-2026","question_number":16,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"C","next_time_hint":"Read the median lines and the box widths.","assessment_style":"formal_assessment","option_labels":["Stall B has a larger IQR, therefore its median waiting time must be larger.","Customers at stall B wait longer on average, and their waiting times are also more varied.","The two stalls have the same spread because both plots are drawn on the same scale.","Stall A is more consistent because its maximum waiting time is smaller than its IQR."],"option_key":[2],"hints":["Compare the medians (centre) and the box widths (spread) separately.","Longer waits on average → higher median; more varied → wider box."],"figure":"myp4_q16_stalls"},"marking":""},{"n":17,"t":"single_choice","p":"The box plot shows the daily number of passengers carried by a taxi on a fixed route for 12 days. Which five-point summary matches the plot?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The box plot shows the daily number of passengers carried by a taxi on a fixed route for 12 days. Which five-point summary matches the plot?"}]},{"type":"image","attrs":{"src":"/exam-figures/myp4/q17_taxi.jpg","alt":"Box plot of passengers per day with an outlier at 52","width":96}}]},"e":"From the plot: smallest value 12 (end of the lower whisker), Q1 ≈ 15.5, median ≈ 18.5, Q3 ≈ 24, and the largest value is the outlier at 52 shown as a dot beyond the upper whisker (which ends near 27). The five-point summary describes the data, so the outlier 52 is included as the maximum.\\n\\nAnswer: D","m":1,"df":"medium","tp":"Box-and-Whisker Plots and Outliers","cfg":{"source":"myp4-full-formal-assessment-2026","question_number":17,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"B","next_time_hint":"The upper whisker is not the largest value here.","assessment_style":"formal_assessment","option_labels":["12, 16, 18.5, 23, 52","12, 15.25, 18.5, 24.5, 52","12, 15.5, 18.5, 24, 27","12, 15.5, 18.5, 24, 52"],"option_key":[4],"hints":["Note the separate dot beyond the whisker — it is a data value.","The five-point summary uses the smallest and largest values, including outliers."],"figure":"myp4_q17_taxi"},"marking":""},{"n":18,"t":"single_choice","p":"A shopkeeper recorded the number of customers arriving each hour on 20 Saturday hours, in the order recorded: 23, 8, 36, 14, 20, 30, 55, 18, 24, 39, 28, 16, 26, 61, 21, 33, 11, 22, 25, 19. How many values in this data set are outliers by the 1.5 × IQR rule?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A shopkeeper recorded the number of customers arriving each hour on 20 Saturday hours, in the order recorded: 23, 8, 36, 14, 20, 30, 55, 18, 24, 39, 28, 16, 26, 61, 21, 33, 11, 22, 25, 19. How many values in this data set are outliers by the 1.5 × IQR rule?"}]}]},"e":"Sorted: 8, 11, 14, 16, 18, 19, 20, 21, 22, 23, 24, 25, 26, 28, 30, 33, 36, 39, 55, 61. n = 20. Lower half 8, 11, 14, 16, 18, 19, 20, 21, 22, 23 → Q1 = (18 + 19) ÷ 2 = 18.5. Median = (23 + 24) ÷ 2 = 23.5. Upper half 24, 25, 26, 28, 30, 33, 36, 39, 55, 61 → Q3 = (30 + 33) ÷ 2 = 31.5. IQR = 31.5 − 18.5 = 13. Upper fence = 31.5 + 1.5 × 13 = 51. Lower fence = 18.5 − 19.5 = −1 (no low outliers possible). Values above 51: 55 and 61 — two outliers.\\n\\nAnswer: C","m":1,"df":"hard","tp":"Box-and-Whisker Plots and Outliers","cfg":{"source":"myp4-full-formal-assessment-2026","question_number":18,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"C","next_time_hint":"Sort first — position order is not size order.","assessment_style":"formal_assessment","option_labels":["10","1","2","0"],"option_key":[3],"hints":["Sort the list before finding the quartiles.","Compute both fences; only values outside them count."]},"marking":""},{"n":19,"t":"single_choice","p":"Two tontine groups record each member’s weekly contribution, in cedis, over 10 weeks. Village A: 20, 25, 30, 35, 40, 45, 50, 55, 60, 105. Village B: 35, 40, 45, 50, 52, 55, 58, 60, 62, 65. Using the 1.5 × IQR rule and the two distributions, which statement is correct?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Two tontine groups record each member’s weekly contribution, in cedis, over 10 weeks. Village A: 20, 25, 30, 35, 40, 45, 50, 55, 60, 105. Village B: 35, 40, 45, 50, 52, 55, 58, 60, 62, 65. Using the 1.5 × IQR rule and the two distributions, which statement is correct?"}]},{"type":"image","attrs":{"src":"/exam-figures/myp4/q19_tontine.jpg","alt":"Box plots of weekly tontine contributions for villages A and B","width":96}}]},"e":"Village A: sorted 20, 25, 30, 35, 40, 45, 50, 55, 60, 105. Lower half 20, 25, 30, 35, 40 → Q1 = 30; median = (40 + 45) ÷ 2 = 42.5; upper half 45, 50, 55, 60, 105 → Q3 = 55; IQR = 25; upper fence = 55 + 1.5 × 25 = 92.5. Since 105 > 92.5, 105 is an outlier. The rule uses 1.5 × IQR, not the range. Village B: lower half 35, 40, 45, 50, 52 → Q1 = 45; median = (52 + 55) ÷ 2 = 53.5; upper half 55, 58, 60, 62, 65 → Q3 = 60; IQR = 15 — tightly clustered, so B is the more consistent group. A’s median (42.5) is not greater than B’s (53.5).\\n\\nAnswer: C","m":1,"df":"hard","tp":"Box-and-Whisker Plots and Outliers","cfg":{"source":"myp4-full-formal-assessment-2026","question_number":19,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"D","next_time_hint":"Apply the fence rule to village A, then compare spreads.","assessment_style":"formal_assessment","option_labels":["105 is an outlier simply because it is the largest value, and that makes A’s median greater than B’s.","105 is not an outlier because it is less than Q3 + 1.5 × range, and A’s median is the higher of the two.","105 is an outlier for village A because it exceeds the upper fence 92.5; village B’s contributions are the more consistent.","Neither village has an outlier because every contribution is a positive number."],"option_key":[3],"hints":["Compute village A’s Q1, Q3 and fence before calling 105 an outlier.","Compare the two distributions’ box widths for consistency."],"figure":"myp4_q19_tontine"},"marking":""}]'::jsonb) as x
